    "text": "To solve for \\( g(4) \\), we need to determine the value of \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = 4 \\). Once we find \\( x \\), we can use it to find \\( g(4) \\).\n\nFirst, let's find \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = 4 \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ f(x) = 2x - 3 \\]\n\nSet \\( f(x) = 4 \\):\n\\[ 2x - 3 = 4 \\]\n\nSolve for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n2x - 3 = 4 \\\\\n2x = 4 + 3 \\\\\n2x = 7 \\\\\nx = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( x = \\frac{7}{2} \\), we need to find \\( g(4) \\). We know that:\n\\[ g(f(x)) = 5 - 4x \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{7}{2} \\) into \\( g(f(x)) \\):\n\\[ g(f\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)) = 5 - 4 \\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right) \\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\\[\ng(f\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)) = 5 - 4 \\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right) \\\\\ng(f\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)) = 5 - 2 \\cdot 7 \\\\\ng(f\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)) = 5 - 14 \\\\\ng(f\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)) = -9\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( g(4) = -9 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-9}\n\\]",
